1.0 INTRODUCTION

1.1 Purpose

This High-Level Design (HLD) document outlines the architecture and design details for the Authenticator App. It breaks down the system requirements into a clear software structure, defining the main components, their interactions, and the technologies involved. This document acts as a guide for development teams to work together and move on to detailed Low-Level Design (LLD) and implementation.

1.2	Scope

The technical scope of this Release 1.0 (MVP) is to create a mobile application for both iOS and Android that includes essential TOTP generation features along with comprehensive backup, import/export, and security capabilities. The deliverables include:

1.	Add accounts via QR scan (otpauth:// URI and otpauth-migration:// format) and manual entry
2.	Offline TOTP generation (default 6 digits, 30 second period)
3.	Secure storage of shared secrets using XChaCha20-Poly1305 AEAD encryption with hardware-backed keys
4.	Local encrypted database storage (SQLite) with integrity verification
5.	List/detail UI with 1 second countdown ring/timer, search, filter, and favorites
6.	Copy code to clipboard with auto clear in 30 seconds
7.	Password-protected encrypted backups (.cdac files) using Argon2id KDF
8.	Backup restore with merge strategies (skip/replace/keep both)
9.	Import accounts from Google Authenticator via otpauth-migration:// QR codes
10.	Export accounts as QR codes for device-to-device transfers
11.	Device security checks (root/jailbreak, emulator, development mode detection)
12.	Database integrity verification using SHA256 checksums
13.	Bulk operations (multi-select delete/export)
14.	Recovery codes generation and verification
15.	Brand-specific icons for popular services
16.	Dark/light theme support
17.	Onboarding screen for first-time users
18.	Basic theming, accessibility (font scaling, high contrast)

1.3 Assumptions and Dependencies
Assumptions: The user's device time is synchronized with world time (NTP). The mobile operating system (iOS/Android) offers solid hardware-backed cryptographic security.

Dependencies: The project relies on the Flutter SDK, the camera plugin for QR scanning, the flutter_secure_storage plugin for secret storage, and the crypto or a similar cryptography package for implementing the TOTP algorithm.
1.4 General Design Constraints
Platform Environment: Must work on Android 8 (API 26)+ and iOS (12+).

Performance: The app must launch and show OTPs in under 2 seconds.
QR Path: QR scan → parse → persist → display must complete in ≤ 3 seconds on supported devices.

Security: All cryptographic secrets must be kept in secure storage backed by hardware (Android Keystore/iOS Keychain). No plaintext secrets should be logged or stored in unsafe places.

Offline Operation: Core OTP generation must work without an internet connection.

Standards Compliance: TOTP generation must meet RFC 6238 requirements. QR code parsing must support the otpauth:// URI scheme.
1.5 Intended Audience
This document is meant for the Project Manager, Software Development Team, Quality Assurance Team, and System Architects involved in the Authenticator project.

