2.0 Design Considerations
	
2.1	Decision Overview
The system is implemented as a single-codebase, cross-platform mobile application in the Flutter framework. The MVVM (Model-View-ViewModel) architecture has been utilized to decouple business logic from the UI, making it easier to test and maintain. This enables a reactive UI where the UI automatically updates when OTPs are refreshed. The option of employing a native development method (Kotlin/Swift) has been eliminated because of the additional development time and effort to maintain two different codebases for the POC.
	
2.2	Reuse and Buy vs. Build Analysis
Non-core functionality is adopted with a "Buy vs. Build" approach to speed up development.

Reuse/Buy (Use Open-Source Libraries): Important pieces such as QR code scanning (camera, google_mlkit_barcode_scanning), secure storage (flutter_secure_storage), and cryptography (crypto, base32) will employ well-tested Flutter plugins. This does not reinvent the wheel but taps into community-tested, secure code.

Build (Custom Development): The essential TOTP generation algorithm, application state management, and all the user interface will be built as custom solutions to satisfy the particular requirements and branding of Authenticator.

2.3	Other Considerations
The design is focused on security first. The selection of flutter_secure_storage guarantees that secrets are stored through platform-native, hardware-backed APIs. The app is built to be stateless about secrets, not holding secrets in memory longer than it needs to.

